.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt DOASEDIT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm doasedit
.Nd edit files with root privileges using an unprivileged editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm doasedit
.Op Fl h | Fl Fl help
.Op Fl -
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
allows editing files that are not editable by the current user.
.Pp
For each file,
.Nm
creates a private, unreadable temporary directory and copies the file
into it, then starts the editor on the copy in the user context.
Once the editor exits, if the temporary file has been modified, the
changes are written back to the original file with the least amount
of privilege possible.
Doas is used only through the fixed command invocations listed under
.Sx "DOAS PERMISSIONS" ,
never through a shell.
.Pp
An initial
.Nm doas Cm dd
probe is always run, before any file is opened, to check that
.Nm doas
is available and permitted.
It does not prove that the invocations needed to write back a
particular file will be permitted.
.Pp
For an existing file, the target's recorded identity is checked again
before the edited copy is written back; if it no longer matches, the
changes are refused rather than written over the unexpected file.
A file that the user may write but not read is updated in place
through the existing inode; that path is not atomic.
Other existing files are replaced through
.Nm doas Cm install ,
which renames a new file into place atomically and preserves the
owner, group and permission bits.
A newly created file is installed with mode 0644.
.Pp
.Nm
refuses to edit:
.Bl -bullet
.It
directories and other non-regular files;
.It
files owned by the calling user;
.It
files that are both readable and writable by the calling user;
.It
a new file whose parent directory exists and is owned by, or writable
by, the calling user.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Prints usage information, then exits.
.It Fl -
Indicates that no more options will be provided.
Everything after the delimiter is treated as a file.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width DOAS_EDITOR
.It Ev DOAS_EDITOR
The program used to edit files.
.It Ev VISUAL
The program used to edit files if
.Ev DOAS_EDITOR
is unset or empty.
.It Ev EDITOR
The program used to edit files if
.Ev DOAS_EDITOR
and
.Ev VISUAL
are unset or empty.
If this is also unset or empty,
.Xr vi 1
is used.
.It Ev TMPDIR
Directory in which the private temporary directory is created;
defaults to
.Pa /tmp .
The value is used as supplied: it is not checked for ownership,
permissions or writability by
.Nm ,
so it must be trusted.
.El
.Pp
The editor command may contain arguments, separated by whitespace.
It is executed directly with
.Xr execvp 3 ;
no shell is involved, so shell quoting, globbing and redirections are
not interpreted.
If the editor starts an existing instance and returns immediately
.Pq typical of some graphical editors ,
.Nm
will compare the file before the edit finished and report it as
unchanged.
Append a command line argument that forces a new instance in that
case.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/doas.conf
.It Pa /etc/doas.conf
.Xr doas 1
configuration file.
After editing it
.Pq or a file below Pa /etc/doas.d/ ,
.Nm
asks
.Nm doas Fl C
to parse the edited copy.
This is a syntax check only; it does not verify that any rule grants
or denies a particular operation, so a syntactically valid change can
still lock the user out.
On a syntax error,
.Nm
offers to edit again, overwrite anyway, or abort.
Whether
.Nm doas
reads files below
.Pa /etc/doas.d/
depends on the local implementation.
.Nm
treats them as configuration for this check, but does not verify that
.Nm doas
uses them.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if help was requested, or if at least one file was edited
successfully or reported unchanged, and 1 otherwise, including when:
.Bl -bullet
.It
no arguments are passed,
.It
invalid arguments are passed,
.It
the program is run as root,
.It
no usable editor is found,
.It
a file cannot be read, validated or written back,
.It
write-back still failed after three attempts.
.El
.Sh DOAS PERMISSIONS
The following doas invocations are used:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm doas Cm dd Ar status=none count=0 of=/dev/null
.Pq unconditional initial probe
.It
.Nm doas Cm cat Ar file
.Pq only when the file is not readable by the user
.It
.Nm doas Cm install Fl o Ar uid Fl g Ar gid Fl m Ar mode Ar tmpfile Ar file
.Pq write-back of a modified existing file
.It
.Nm doas Cm install Fl m Ar 0644 Ar tmpfile Ar file
.Pq write-back of a newly created file
.It
.Nm doas Fl C Ar tmpfile
.Pq syntax check of an edited doas configuration
.El
.Pp
Write-back through
.Nm doas Cm install
is retried after a failure, up to three attempts in total, which
allows for mistyped passwords.
When an unreadable file is copied with
.Nm doas Cm cat ,
a detected concurrent change causes another copy attempt.
Using
.Cm persist
in
.Xr doas.conf 5
avoids repeated password prompts.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
never runs an editor, or any other program, with elevated
privileges; privilege is confined to the fixed doas invocations
listed above.
The real security boundary is
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
and its included files where the local implementation supports them:
a broad or blanket rule that permits these invocations also permits
other privileged operations.
.Pp
Temporary files are created with
.Xr mkdtemp 3
in a private directory.
The directory named by
.Ev TMPDIR
is trusted and is not validated.
Files are opened with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
and their identity
.Pq device, inode, mode, owner, group, size and modification time
is recorded before editing and checked again before write-back; if
the target changed, the write is refused.
.Pp
On the
.Nm doas Cm install
path, the target is replaced by a rename in its directory, so a
symbolic link swapped in for the final component is replaced rather
than followed.
This preserves ownership, group and permission bits, including
setuid and setgid bits, but timestamps, ACLs, extended attributes and
file flags may be lost, and hard links are broken.
However,
.Nm install
reopens the temporary source by pathname, so the temporary directory
must not be writable by an attacker.
The target identity check is made immediately before the install, not
atomically with it, and there is no post-install identity check.
The direct-write path used for write-only files updates the existing
inode in place and is not atomic: an error or interruption can leave
a partially written file.
.Sh LOCALE
The interface of
.Nm
is hard-coded English and the program does not call
.Xr setlocale 3 ,
so it runs in the C locale.
Setting
.Ev LANG ,
.Ev LANGUAGE ,
.Ev LC_ALL ,
.Ev LC_MESSAGES
or any other locale variable does not change its messages, prompts or
diagnostics.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr doas 1 ,
.Xr doas.conf 5
